1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,580
At a plague

2
00:00:00,580 --> 00:00:02,080
cemetery in London,

3
00:00:02,080 --> 00:00:03,100
archaeologists found

4
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:05,240
one hundred and eighty-one coins.

5
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:05,740
They lay

6
00:00:05,740 --> 00:00:06,200
in the grave

7
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:06,760
of a woman

8
00:00:06,760 --> 00:00:07,360
who died

9
00:00:07,360 --> 00:00:08,580
during the epidemic.

10
00:00:08,580 --> 00:00:09,300
We do not know

11
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:10,020
why the money

12
00:00:10,020 --> 00:00:11,060
remained with her.

13
00:00:11,060 --> 00:00:12,180
Perhaps people feared

14
00:00:12,180 --> 00:00:13,100
infection if they

15
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:13,820
removed the dead

16
00:00:13,820 --> 00:00:14,760
woman's clothing.

17
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:15,500
But the same

18
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:16,320
cemetery holds

19
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:17,920
another important detail.

20
00:00:17,920 --> 00:00:18,960
Even in the communal

21
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:20,500
graves, bodies were carefully

22
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:21,980
laid out in rows.

23
00:00:21,980 --> 00:00:23,060
Fear did not bring

24
00:00:23,060 --> 00:00:23,700
all essential

25
00:00:23,700 --> 00:00:24,980
work to a stop.

26
00:00:24,980 --> 00:00:26,040
Someone continued

27
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:26,640
to do it.

28
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:28,240
The cemetery was established

29
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:28,900
near the Tower

30
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:30,060
of London when plague

31
00:00:30,060 --> 00:00:30,740
reached the city

32
00:00:30,740 --> 00:00:32,620
in late thirteen forty-eight.

33
00:00:32,620 --> 00:00:33,460
Ordinary burial

34
00:00:33,460 --> 00:00:34,220
grounds were running

35
00:00:34,220 --> 00:00:35,380
out of space.

36
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:36,380
People did not know

37
00:00:36,380 --> 00:00:37,700
what caused the disease.

38
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:38,640
They had to decide

39
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:39,340
whether to open

40
00:00:39,340 --> 00:00:39,640
the door

41
00:00:39,640 --> 00:00:40,520
to a neighbor,

42
00:00:40,520 --> 00:00:41,240
care for a sick

43
00:00:41,240 --> 00:00:42,300
relative, or go

44
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:43,220
to market.

45
00:00:43,220 --> 00:00:44,220
Lives depended

46
00:00:44,220 --> 00:00:45,220
on those decisions.

47
00:00:45,220 --> 00:00:45,820
But people

48
00:00:45,820 --> 00:00:46,960
often had no reliable

49
00:00:46,960 --> 00:00:47,680
way to tell

50
00:00:47,680 --> 00:00:49,040
which choice was right.

51
00:00:49,040 --> 00:00:49,980
How do you protect

52
00:00:49,980 --> 00:00:51,060
yourself from a danger

53
00:00:51,060 --> 00:00:52,020
you cannot recognize?

54
00:00:52,020 --> 00:00:53,020
And how do you

55
00:00:53,020 --> 00:00:53,840
preserve a city

56
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:54,520
when everyone

57
00:00:54,520 --> 00:00:55,420
is afraid to come

58
00:00:55,420 --> 00:00:56,920
close to another person?

59
00:00:56,920 --> 00:00:57,860
Medieval Europe

60
00:00:57,860 --> 00:00:58,780
was not a collection

61
00:00:58,780 --> 00:01:00,300
of isolated villages.

62
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:01,760
Cities needed grain,

63
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:03,060
cloth, and metal.

64
00:01:03,060 --> 00:01:03,900
Ships sailed

65
00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:05,480
between ports, merchants

66
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:06,320
carried goods across

67
00:01:06,320 --> 00:01:07,500
borders, and people

68
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:08,360
traveled for work

69
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:09,560
and returned home.

70
00:01:09,560 --> 00:01:10,260
This network

71
00:01:10,260 --> 00:01:11,240
made life possible

72
00:01:11,240 --> 00:01:12,120
even in places

73
00:01:12,120 --> 00:01:13,100
that could not produce

74
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:14,360
everything they needed.

75
00:01:14,360 --> 00:01:15,460
It also created

76
00:01:15,460 --> 00:01:16,880
routes for infection.

77
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:18,180
Animals and parasites

78
00:01:18,180 --> 00:01:19,000
moved alongside

79
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:20,080
people and cargo.

80
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:21,480
The disease could cross

81
00:01:21,480 --> 00:01:22,920
distances that no single

82
00:01:22,920 --> 00:01:23,960
sick person traveled

83
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:25,220
from beginning to end.

84
00:01:25,220 --> 00:01:25,840
By the end

85
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:27,240
of thirteen forty-seven,

86
00:01:27,240 --> 00:01:28,360
the epidemic was spreading

87
00:01:28,360 --> 00:01:29,820
across the Mediterranean.

88
00:01:29,820 --> 00:01:30,840
The following year,

89
00:01:30,840 --> 00:01:31,300
it swept

90
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:32,580
through Florence, reached

91
00:01:32,580 --> 00:01:33,560
England, and made

92
00:01:33,560 --> 00:01:34,600
its way to London.

93
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:35,380
The disaster

94
00:01:35,380 --> 00:01:36,500
also affected other

95
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:37,420
parts of Eurasia

96
00:01:37,420 --> 00:01:38,640
and North Africa.

97
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:39,700
Looking at a map,

98
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:40,240
it is tempting

99
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:41,180
to draw a single

100
00:01:41,180 --> 00:01:42,020
line and trace

101
00:01:42,020 --> 00:01:43,420
the disease's movement.

102
00:01:43,420 --> 00:01:44,280
In reality,

103
00:01:44,280 --> 00:01:44,880
there were many

104
00:01:44,880 --> 00:01:46,880
routes and separate outbreaks.

105
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:48,340
Neighboring regions could experience

106
00:01:48,340 --> 00:01:49,360
them very differently.

107
00:01:49,360 --> 00:01:50,780
News of the disaster

108
00:01:50,780 --> 00:01:51,700
could arrive before

109
00:01:51,700 --> 00:01:53,020
the disease itself.

110
00:01:53,020 --> 00:01:53,800
People in England

111
00:01:53,800 --> 00:01:54,760
knew of the approaching

112
00:01:54,760 --> 00:01:55,580
danger before

113
00:01:55,580 --> 00:01:57,140
the first local outbreaks.

114
00:01:57,140 --> 00:01:57,880
But the warning

115
00:01:57,880 --> 00:01:58,720
did not answer

116
00:01:58,720 --> 00:01:59,860
the central question.

117
00:01:59,860 --> 00:02:00,620
What should they

118
00:02:00,620 --> 00:02:01,520
do now?

119
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:02,380
Behind every

120
00:02:02,380 --> 00:02:03,160
point on the map

121
00:02:03,160 --> 00:02:03,760
was a place

122
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:05,280
that still needed food.

123
00:02:05,280 --> 00:02:06,400
An order could close

124
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:07,240
the gates.

125
00:02:07,240 --> 00:02:07,680
An order

126
00:02:07,680 --> 00:02:08,900
alone could not feed

127
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:09,920
the people inside.

128
00:02:09,920 --> 00:02:10,880
So, authorities

129
00:02:10,880 --> 00:02:11,760
faced a problem

130
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:13,160
with no simple solution.

131
00:02:13,160 --> 00:02:14,360
How to limit

132
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:15,940
dangerous connections without

133
00:02:15,940 --> 00:02:16,960
depriving a city

134
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:17,500
of the things

135
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:18,860
it needed to survive?

136
00:02:18,860 --> 00:02:20,100
For someone who fell

137
00:02:20,100 --> 00:02:21,320
ill, that vast

138
00:02:21,320 --> 00:02:22,980
geography meant nothing.

139
00:02:22,980 --> 00:02:24,120
A fever began

140
00:02:24,120 --> 00:02:25,900
followed by severe weakness.

141
00:02:25,900 --> 00:02:26,940
In the bubonic

142
00:02:26,940 --> 00:02:27,940
form of plague,

143
00:02:27,940 --> 00:02:28,680
the lymph nodes

144
00:02:28,680 --> 00:02:30,320
became painfully swollen,

145
00:02:30,320 --> 00:02:31,260
often in the groin

146
00:02:31,260 --> 00:02:32,560
or under the arms.

147
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:34,180
Sometimes the infection affected

148
00:02:34,180 --> 00:02:35,740
the blood or lungs.

149
00:02:35,740 --> 00:02:36,820
Today, a doctor

150
00:02:36,820 --> 00:02:37,920
knows what infection

151
00:02:37,920 --> 00:02:39,220
such symptoms may indicate.

152
00:02:39,220 --> 00:02:40,420
In the fourteenth

153
00:02:40,420 --> 00:02:41,380
century, people

154
00:02:41,380 --> 00:02:43,200
looked for different explanations.

155
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:44,080
An imbalance within

156
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:45,280
the body, corrupted

157
00:02:45,280 --> 00:02:46,640
air, or the influence

158
00:02:46,640 --> 00:02:48,080
of heavenly bodies.

159
00:02:48,080 --> 00:02:49,060
Religious beliefs

160
00:02:49,060 --> 00:02:49,960
linked the disaster

161
00:02:49,960 --> 00:02:51,420
to punishment for sin.

162
00:02:51,420 --> 00:02:52,560
If someone believes

163
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:54,000
the air is dangerous,

164
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:55,420
fragrant herbs and incense

165
00:02:55,420 --> 00:02:57,000
seem like reasonable protection.

166
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:57,740
They act

167
00:02:57,740 --> 00:02:58,700
according to the knowledge

168
00:02:58,700 --> 00:02:59,840
available to them.

169
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:00,400
The problem

170
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:01,520
is that this knowledge

171
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:02,440
does not explain

172
00:03:02,440 --> 00:03:03,120
what is happening.

173
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:04,220
The cause

174
00:03:04,220 --> 00:03:05,640
was a bacterium,

175
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:07,080
Yersinia pestis.

176
00:03:07,080 --> 00:03:08,320
It persists in natural

177
00:03:08,320 --> 00:03:09,540
reservoirs among small

178
00:03:09,540 --> 00:03:10,900
mammals and their fleas.

179
00:03:10,900 --> 00:03:12,060
A person can become

180
00:03:12,060 --> 00:03:13,140
infected through the bite

181
00:03:13,140 --> 00:03:13,780
of an infected

182
00:03:13,780 --> 00:03:14,920
flea or through

183
00:03:14,920 --> 00:03:15,960
contact with infected

184
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:16,920
animal tissue.

185
00:03:16,920 --> 00:03:18,580
In the pneumonic form,

186
00:03:18,580 --> 00:03:19,760
transmission through respiratory

187
00:03:19,760 --> 00:03:20,920
droplets is possible

188
00:03:20,920 --> 00:03:21,960
during close contact

189
00:03:21,960 --> 00:03:23,260
with a sick person.

190
00:03:23,260 --> 00:03:24,320
Scientists are still

191
00:03:24,320 --> 00:03:25,680
investigating which routes

192
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:26,860
predominated in different

193
00:03:26,860 --> 00:03:27,960
medieval outbreaks.

194
00:03:27,960 --> 00:03:28,940
They also debate

195
00:03:28,940 --> 00:03:29,920
the role of parasites

196
00:03:29,920 --> 00:03:30,900
that live on humans.

197
00:03:30,900 --> 00:03:32,320
A single diagram

198
00:03:32,320 --> 00:03:33,300
featuring a rat

199
00:03:33,300 --> 00:03:34,740
cannot confidently explain

200
00:03:34,740 --> 00:03:36,000
the entire pandemic.

201
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:37,060
But people around

202
00:03:37,060 --> 00:03:37,980
someone who was ill

203
00:03:37,980 --> 00:03:39,620
saw a simpler sequence.

204
00:03:39,620 --> 00:03:40,580
One person became

205
00:03:40,580 --> 00:03:41,600
sick, and then

206
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:42,400
someone nearby

207
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:43,800
became sick, too.

208
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:44,560
Even without

209
00:03:44,560 --> 00:03:46,500
knowledge of bacteria, closeness

210
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:48,020
began to inspire fear.

211
00:03:48,020 --> 00:03:49,420
And so, a question

212
00:03:49,420 --> 00:03:50,140
about disease

213
00:03:50,140 --> 00:03:51,200
became a question

214
00:03:51,200 --> 00:03:52,580
about another person.

215
00:03:52,580 --> 00:03:53,360
When the sick

216
00:03:53,360 --> 00:03:54,580
person was a stranger,

217
00:03:54,580 --> 00:03:55,600
you could step away.

218
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:56,520
But what could you

219
00:03:56,520 --> 00:03:57,360
do when the person

220
00:03:57,360 --> 00:03:58,120
who fell ill

221
00:03:58,120 --> 00:03:58,880
lived with you?

222
00:03:58,880 --> 00:04:00,600
In Florence, Giovanni

223
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:02,540
Boccaccio described this change.

224
00:04:02,540 --> 00:04:03,380
In the introduction

225
00:04:03,380 --> 00:04:04,500
to the Decameron,

226
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:05,300
he recounts how

227
00:04:05,300 --> 00:04:06,460
people tried to survive

228
00:04:06,460 --> 00:04:07,580
the epidemic.

229
00:04:07,580 --> 00:04:09,360
Authorities removed refuse,

230
00:04:09,360 --> 00:04:09,900
barred sick

231
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:10,620
people from entering

232
00:04:10,620 --> 00:04:11,780
the city, and issued

233
00:04:11,780 --> 00:04:13,520
advice on staying healthy.

234
00:04:13,520 --> 00:04:14,660
Measures were taken,

235
00:04:14,660 --> 00:04:15,400
but the disaster

236
00:04:15,400 --> 00:04:16,780
could not be stopped.

237
00:04:16,780 --> 00:04:17,540
Some limited

238
00:04:17,540 --> 00:04:18,880
their contact with others,

239
00:04:18,880 --> 00:04:19,460
shut themselves

240
00:04:19,460 --> 00:04:20,420
away with a small

241
00:04:20,420 --> 00:04:21,420
circle of people,

242
00:04:21,420 --> 00:04:23,060
and tried to live moderately.

243
00:04:23,060 --> 00:04:24,560
Others drank, visited

244
00:04:24,560 --> 00:04:26,520
taverns, and sought entertainment.

245
00:04:26,520 --> 00:04:27,660
Some left the city.

246
00:04:27,660 --> 00:04:28,760
This is a work

247
00:04:28,760 --> 00:04:29,660
of literature,

248
00:04:29,660 --> 00:04:30,280
not a census

249
00:04:30,280 --> 00:04:30,640
of how

250
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:32,220
every Florentine behaved.

251
00:04:32,220 --> 00:04:32,920
But it reveals

252
00:04:32,920 --> 00:04:33,920
the fear of people

253
00:04:33,920 --> 00:04:35,020
who no longer knew

254
00:04:35,020 --> 00:04:35,920
which of their habits

255
00:04:35,920 --> 00:04:36,820
were safe.

256
00:04:36,820 --> 00:04:37,800
Particularly painful

257
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:38,600
are the passages

258
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:39,860
about relatives who left

259
00:04:39,860 --> 00:04:41,340
the sick without help.

260
00:04:41,340 --> 00:04:42,360
Boccaccio describes

261
00:04:42,360 --> 00:04:43,400
the breaking of bonds

262
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:44,540
people usually rely

263
00:04:44,540 --> 00:04:45,820
on in a crisis.

264
00:04:45,820 --> 00:04:46,380
A home,

265
00:04:46,380 --> 00:04:47,060
where a person

266
00:04:47,060 --> 00:04:47,780
should have been cared

267
00:04:47,780 --> 00:04:48,720
for, could become

268
00:04:48,720 --> 00:04:50,260
a place of isolation.

269
00:04:50,260 --> 00:04:50,820
Not everyone

270
00:04:50,820 --> 00:04:51,420
had the means

271
00:04:51,420 --> 00:04:51,940
to leave.

272
00:04:51,940 --> 00:04:53,260
Waiting out the disaster

273
00:04:53,260 --> 00:04:54,020
in the countryside

274
00:04:54,020 --> 00:04:55,540
required money, shelter,

275
00:04:55,540 --> 00:04:56,880
and people to provide

276
00:04:56,880 --> 00:04:58,060
for everyday life.

277
00:04:58,060 --> 00:04:59,600
In the Decameron itself,

278
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:00,360
the group that leaves

279
00:05:00,360 --> 00:05:02,080
Florence has those resources.

280
00:05:02,080 --> 00:05:02,980
But if everyone

281
00:05:02,980 --> 00:05:04,120
leaves, who will bring

282
00:05:04,120 --> 00:05:05,280
water to the sick?

283
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:06,520
Who will bake bread?

284
00:05:06,520 --> 00:05:07,100
Who will take

285
00:05:07,100 --> 00:05:08,060
away the dead?

286
00:05:08,060 --> 00:05:08,800
This was one

287
00:05:08,800 --> 00:05:09,560
of the epidemic's

288
00:05:09,560 --> 00:05:11,080
hardest contradictions.

289
00:05:11,080 --> 00:05:11,740
Staying away

290
00:05:11,740 --> 00:05:12,340
from the sick

291
00:05:12,340 --> 00:05:13,460
could reduce the danger

292
00:05:13,460 --> 00:05:14,540
to an individual,

293
00:05:14,540 --> 00:05:15,100
yet someone

294
00:05:15,100 --> 00:05:16,060
still had to care

295
00:05:16,060 --> 00:05:17,900
for those who could no longer help themselves.

296
00:05:17,900 --> 00:05:18,740
And when we

297
00:05:18,740 --> 00:05:19,600
return to the London

298
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:20,820
burials, it becomes

299
00:05:20,820 --> 00:05:21,840
clear that a story

300
00:05:21,840 --> 00:05:22,600
in which everyone

301
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:24,400
fled would be incomplete.

302
00:05:24,400 --> 00:05:24,960
At the East

303
00:05:24,960 --> 00:05:26,180
Smithfield Cemetery,

304
00:05:26,180 --> 00:05:26,960
there were individual

305
00:05:26,960 --> 00:05:27,820
graves as well

306
00:05:27,820 --> 00:05:29,460
as communal burial trenches.

307
00:05:29,460 --> 00:05:30,040
It is easy

308
00:05:30,040 --> 00:05:30,500
to imagine

309
00:05:30,500 --> 00:05:31,620
the latter as chaotic

310
00:05:31,620 --> 00:05:33,240
pits, but archaeologists

311
00:05:33,240 --> 00:05:35,260
found carefully arranged bodies.

312
00:05:35,260 --> 00:05:35,840
The cemetery

313
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:37,840
itself required organization.

314
00:05:37,840 --> 00:05:38,800
Land had to be set

315
00:05:38,800 --> 00:05:40,400
aside, graves dug,

316
00:05:40,400 --> 00:05:40,740
the dead

317
